```minizinc
% Climb a staircase of n = 20 steps
% at each move you can take between m1 = 3 and m2 = 5 steps.
% We model up to maxMoves = 20 moves, filling with 0 once we reach the top.

int: n = 20;
int: minStep = 3;
int: maxStep = 5;
int: maxMoves = n;

array[1..maxMoves] of var 0..maxStep: steps;

% 1) The total of all moves must be exactly n.
% 2) Each move is either 0 (no move) or at least minStep.
% 3) Once we take a 0-move, all subsequent moves must also be 0.
constraint
  sum(i in 1..maxMoves)(steps[i]) = n /\
  forall(i in 1..maxMoves)(
    steps[i] = 0 \/ steps[i] >= minStep
  ) /\
  forall(i in 1..maxMoves-1)(
    steps[i] = 0 -> steps[i+1] = 0
  );

solve satisfy;

output [
  "{",
    "\"steps\": [" ++
      join(",", [ show(steps[i]) | i in 1..maxMoves ]) ++
    "]",
  "}"
];
```

Explanation of key parts:

• `steps[i]` is in 0..5.  
• `steps[i] = 0 \/ steps[i] >= 3` ensures each non‐zero move is between 3 and 5.  
• `steps[i] = 0 -> steps[i+1] = 0` forces zeros to form a suffix once the staircase is reached.  
• `sum(steps) = 20` makes the total climbed equal to 20.  
• The output prints the 20‐element list `steps` as JSON.

