int: n = 6;
array[1..n] of int: demands = [4, 8, 10, 7, 12, 4];

% S[i] = number of buses starting service at slot i (and running also in the next slot, cyclically)
array[1..n] of var 0..sum(demands): S;

% x[i] = total buses available in slot i
array[1..n] of var 0..sum(demands): x;

% Compute coverage and enforce demand
constraint
  forall(i in 1..n) (
    let { int: prev = if i == 1 then n else i - 1 } in
      x[i] = S[i] + S[prev]
  )
  /\
  forall(i in 1..n) (
    x[i] >= demands[i]
  );

% Minimize the total number of distinct buses used
var int: total_buses = sum(i in 1..n)(S[i]);
solve minimize total_buses;

output [
  "{",
    "\"x\": [" ++ join(",", [show(x[i]) | i in 1..n]) ++ "]",
  "}"
];
```

Explanation of the model:

1. We have 6 consecutive 4-hour slots in a 24-hour period (n = 6).  
2. `demands[i]` is the required number of buses in slot `i`.  
3. A bus can serve two successive slots (8 hours straight), so if it “starts” at slot `i`, it will also cover slot `i+1` (with wrap-around from 6 to 1).  
4. `S[i]` counts how many buses start service at slot `i`.  
5. `x[i]` = `S[i]` + `S[prev(i)]` gives the total buses operating in slot `i`.  
6. We ensure `x[i] >= demands[i]` for each slot.  
7. The objective is to minimize the sum of all starts `sum(S[i])`, which equals the total number of distinct buses required.  
8. The output prints the array `x` in JSON format.

